      title: "The Last Wars for the West",
      body: `## The Plains peoples

In 1865, most of the vast region between the Missouri River and the Rocky Mountains was still controlled by Native nations. On the Great Plains, peoples such as the **Lakota** (Western Sioux), Cheyenne, Arapaho, Comanche, and Kiowa had, as you saw in AMH 110, built powerful societies around the horse and the bison. They were formidable military powers. Within twenty-five years, they had been defeated, confined to reservations, and stripped of most of their land.

## Wars and treaties

The Civil War years brought violence to the Plains. In November 1864, at **Sand Creek** in Colorado, militia under Colonel John Chivington attacked a peaceful Cheyenne and Arapaho village flying an American flag and a white flag, killing about 150 to 200 people, most of them women, children, and old men, and mutilating the bodies. A congressional committee condemned the massacre.

In the Powder River country of Wyoming and Montana, the Lakota leader **Red Cloud** fought to close the Bozeman Trail, which ran through Lakota hunting grounds to the Montana gold fields. In December 1866, warriors annihilated a detachment of eighty-one soldiers under Captain William Fetterman. Red Cloud's War ended in a rare Native victory: in the **Treaty of Fort Laramie** of 1868, the United States abandoned its forts on the trail and recognized a Great Sioux Reservation that included the **Black Hills**, sacred to the Lakota, "for the absolute and undisturbed use and occupation" of the Lakota.

In 1869, President Grant announced a "**Peace Policy**," placing reservations under the management of church denominations, which would teach Native peoples to farm and convert them to Christianity. But the policy assumed that Native peoples would give up their way of life and stay on reservations, and it was backed by force against those who refused.

## The Black Hills and Little Bighorn

In 1874, an army expedition led by Lieutenant Colonel **George Armstrong Custer** confirmed rumors of gold in the Black Hills. Thousands of miners poured in, in violation of the Fort Laramie Treaty. When the Lakota refused to sell the Hills, the government ordered all Lakota to report to the reservation agencies by January 31, 1876, and sent the army against those who did not.

On June 25, 1876, Custer's Seventh Cavalry attacked a great encampment of Lakota, Cheyenne, and Arapaho, perhaps 7,000 people, on the **Little Bighorn River** in Montana, gathered around the Hunkpapa Lakota spiritual leader **Sitting Bull**. Custer divided his regiment, and the warriors, led by **Crazy Horse** and others, surrounded and killed Custer and all of the roughly 210 men with him; in all, about 260 soldiers died. The news reached the East during the celebrations of the nation's centennial. "Custer's Last Stand" became a national legend of heroic sacrifice. For the Lakota and Cheyenne, it was a great victory—and their last. The army pursued the bands relentlessly through the following winter. Crazy Horse surrendered in May 1877 and was killed in custody that September. In 1877 Congress simply took the Black Hills. (In 1980, the Supreme Court held that the taking had been illegal and awarded the Lakota compensation; they have refused the money, which has grown to over a billion dollars, insisting on the return of the land.)

## Chief Joseph and the Nez Perce

In 1877, the army ordered the **Nez Perce** of Oregon's Wallowa Valley onto a reservation in Idaho. Some young warriors killed several settlers, and the bands led by **Chief Joseph** and others fled, hoping to reach Canada. Over about four months, some 800 Nez Perce, including women and children, traveled more than 1,100 miles through Idaho, Wyoming, and Montana, outfighting and outmaneuvering pursuing armies, before they were surrounded in the Bear Paw Mountains, about forty miles from the Canadian border. Joseph's surrender speech, as recorded by an army officer, became famous: "Hear me, my chiefs! I am tired; my heart is sick and sad. From where the sun now stands I will fight no more forever." Historians have questioned whether the words are entirely his. Despite promises that they could return home, the Nez Perce were sent to Indian Territory, where many died of disease.

## The destruction of the bison

The military defeat of the Plains peoples was accompanied by the near-extermination of the **bison**, on which their way of life depended. In the 1870s and 1880s, commercial hunters armed with high-powered rifles slaughtered bison by the millions for their hides, which were used to make industrial machine belts and leather, leaving the carcasses to rot. Army officers encouraged the slaughter, knowing that it would force Native peoples onto reservations. From perhaps 30 million in the early nineteenth century, the bison were reduced to fewer than a thousand by the late 1880s.

## Geronimo and the Southwest

In the Southwest, Apache bands resisted confinement on reservations for years. The Chiricahua Apache leader **Geronimo** led raids from Mexico into Arizona and New Mexico until 1886, when he surrendered to a force of 5,000 soldiers—a quarter of the U.S. Army—pursuing a band of a few dozen. Geronimo and his people, including Apache scouts who had served the army, were imprisoned in Florida and Alabama, and later at Fort Sill in Oklahoma, where he died in 1909, still a prisoner of war.

## Wounded Knee

In 1889, a Paiute prophet in Nevada named **Wovoka** began preaching a new religion, the **Ghost Dance**. If Native peoples lived peacefully and performed the dance, he taught, the dead would return, the bison would be restored, and the whites would disappear. The religion spread rapidly among the desperate peoples of the Plains. On the Lakota reservations, alarmed agents called for troops. In December 1890, Indian police sent to arrest Sitting Bull killed him in a confrontation at his cabin.

On December 29, 1890, at **Wounded Knee Creek** in South Dakota, soldiers of the Seventh Cavalry—Custer's old regiment—surrounded a band of Lakota under Spotted Elk (Big Foot) and began to disarm them. A shot was fired, and the soldiers opened fire with rifles and rapid-firing Hotchkiss guns. Some 250 to 300 Lakota were killed, many of them women and children, some of them pursued for miles. Twenty soldiers received the Medal of Honor. Wounded Knee is usually taken to mark the end of the Indian wars.

> **Pause and consider.** Why did the destruction of the bison matter as much as military defeat to the Plains peoples?`,
